    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the info. i think i saw some 10-10-10 stuff at the hardware store. if i do end up getting a high phosphorus one, how much should i actually be putting down? i don't want to burn the grass if i overdo it.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For a proper phosphorus application rate, check the label on the fertilizer bag for the recommended dosage per 1,000 square feet. For slow-release forms, typical rates are between 5 to 10 pounds of actual phosphorus (P2O5) per 1,000 square feet, applied once or twice a year during the growing season. However, it's critical to avoid excessive application, as this can harm the grass. Overfertilization can lead to leaf scorch, reduced plant growth, and even ecosystem damage. For best results, perform a soil test to confirm phosphorus needs, check with a local nursery or extension service, and follow the specific guidance provided for your area and lawn type.",
